{"parentUuid":"f21f2d6d-0792-4c4f-a358-7a682324ff52","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"be2a55ac-7180-4afe-8e1f-07c1f28bd2fe","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01N3vNFmx9LWR8Jwt5QjpBQe","type":"tool_result","content":"title:\tFeature Request: Support for non-keyring secret storage (Headless/CI  environments)\nstate:\tOPEN\nauthor:\tdigiSal\nlabels:\tenhancement\ncomments:\t2\nassignees:\tpeyton-alt\nprojects:\t\nmilestone:\t\nnumber:\t1036\n--\n### Problem or use case\n\nThe Entire CLI currently relies on the system keyring (via the Secret Service D-Bus\n     interface) to store authentication tokens. On headless Linux environments (SSH, WSL, Docker,\n     or CI/CD runners), this often fails because no unlocked \"default\" or \"login\" collection is\n     available, preventing users from logging in.\nMany users operate in environments where `gnome-keyring-daemon` or `dbus` are not running\n      or are difficult to configure. Providing a standard way to bypass the system keyring would\n      make Entire much more accessible for remote development and automation.\n\n\n### Desired behavior\n\n```shell\nWhen running in a headless or non-GUI environment, entire should provide an alternative to the\n  system keyring for persistent authentication.\n```\n\n### Proposed solution\n\nTo support headless environments more gracefully, I propose adding a fallback or\n      configurable secret store backend:\n    \n     Allow users to specify a local file for secret storage via an\n      environment variable (e.g., `ENTIRE_SECRETS_PATH=~/.entire/credentials`) or a flag.\n     **Environment Variable Fallback:** Allow providing the authentication token directly via\n      an environment variable (e.g., `ENTIRE_AUTH_TOKEN`) to support non-interactive CI/CD\n      workflows.\n     **Plain-text Fallback:** A `--no-keyring` flag that stores the token in plain text\n      within the `.entire` directory (with a warning about security implications).\n\n### Alternatives or workarounds\n\nCurrently, I have to manually launch `dbus-launch` and pipe a password into\n      `gnome-keyring-daemon --unlock`, which is not ideal for many workflows.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"106f75c4-7fa5-44e2-848a-fe500bcd9a77","timestamp":"2026-05-06T16:46:32.890Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"title:\tFeature Request: Support for non-keyring secret storage (Headless/CI  environments)\nstate:\tOPEN\nauthor:\tdigiSal\nlabels:\tenhancement\ncomments:\t2\nassignees:\tpeyton-alt\nprojects:\t\nmilestone:\t\nnumber:\t1036\n--\n### Problem or use case\n\nThe Entire CLI currently relies on the system keyring (via the Secret Service D-Bus\n     interface) to store authentication tokens. On headless Linux environments (SSH, WSL, Docker,\n     or CI/CD runners), this often fails because no unlocked \"default\" or \"login\" collection is\n     available, preventing users from logging in.\nMany users operate in environments where `gnome-keyring-daemon` or `dbus` are not running\n      or are difficult to configure. Providing a standard way to bypass the system keyring would\n      make Entire much more accessible for remote development and automation.\n\n\n### Desired behavior\n\n```shell\nWhen running in a headless or non-GUI environment, entire should provide an alternative to the\n  system keyring for persistent authentication.\n```\n\n### Proposed solution\n\nTo support headless environments more gracefully, I propose adding a fallback or\n      configurable secret store backend:\n    \n     Allow users to specify a local file for secret storage via an\n      environment variable (e.g., `ENTIRE_SECRETS_PATH=~/.entire/credentials`) or a flag.\n     **Environment Variable Fallback:** Allow providing the authentication token directly via\n      an environment variable (e.g., `ENTIRE_AUTH_TOKEN`) to support non-interactive CI/CD\n      workflows.\n     **Plain-text Fallback:** A `--no-keyring` flag that stores the token in plain text\n      within the `