{"parentUuid":"9a89dd72-5847-41cb-9831-3e56f12b4b58","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01QjyTBMygv2VRVhSq7dMEwh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have everything I need. Here's the review.\n\n## Review of PR #1127 against issue #1036\n\n### Scope match\n\nIssue #1036 requested three options: env-var token, file storage, and a `--no-keyring` plaintext flag. The PR delivers the first two and **intentionally drops the third** — `ENTIRE_SECRETS_PATH` is plaintext JSON anyway, so the `--no-keyring` flag would be redundant surface area for the same security model. That's a defensible scope reduction.\n\n### Size justification (969 +/37 -)\n\n| Category | Lines | Files |\n|---|---|---|\n| Production code | ~395 | 7 |\n| Tests | ~578 | 5 |\n\nThe 1.5:1 test-to-prod ratio is appropriate for credential-handling code. The bulk is one new module: `file_store.go` (188 LOC) + `env.go` (35 LOC). The rest is the `TokenInfo` refactor rippling through the four auth-aware commands (`status`, `login`, `logout`, `revoke`) so they message correctly when the token comes from env vs. file vs. keyring. **Size is proportional to scope, not bloat.**\n\n`★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────`\n- `GetToken` is now a thin wrapper over `GetTokenInfo` (`store.go:54-61`). This is the right shape — one canonical lookup path with source attribution, and callers that don't need attribution still get a clean string API.\n- `file_store.go` does atomic temp+rename writes with `0o600` perms applied **before** the rename and re-asserted after. It also rejects group-readable files on read (`file_store.go:66-68`). That's defense-in-depth — a partial write or pre-existing loose-perms file can't silently leak.\n- The env-token revoke tests (`auth_test.go:484-553`) protect against a subtle footgun: if a user runs `auth revoke --current` while `ENTIRE_AUTH_TOKEN` is set, you must NOT also wipe their file/keyring credential — those are separate identities. The test asserts `store.deleted` stays false. This is the kind of edge case that bites people in production.\n`─────────────────────────────────────────────────`\n\n### What's good\n\n- **Precedence is clear and tested**: env > file > keyring, in both code (`store.go:65-91`) and README.\n- **Security boundaries**: absolute-path enforcement, `~/` expansion done in code (not by shell), directory-path rejection, perm checks on read, atomic write.\n- **Versioned JSON schema** (`fileStoreVersion = 1`) — forward-compatible.\n- **Source-aware UX**: `auth status`, `logout`, and `revoke --current` all behave correctly when token comes from env (don't try to \"delete\" an env var; tell the user to unset it).\n- **Integration test** (`integration_test/login_test.go:113-198`) exercises the full `entire login` → file write flow with permission and JSON-shape assertions.\n- **Tests are non-redundant** — I scanned for overlap; each test exercises a distinct branch (whitespace env, empty save, malformed JSON, group-readable file, env-shadow warning, env self-revoke, etc.).\n\n### Minor things worth raising (not blockers)\n\n1. **Corrupt-file logout footgun**: if a user's `ENTIRE_SECRETS_PATH` file becomes malformed JSON, `deleteFileToken` (`file_store.go:169-188`) propagates the parse error → logout fails. Could either log-and-overwrite during delete, or leave a one-line comment documenting the manual-fix expectation. Not a regression (keyring-locked has the same shape), but worth a thought.\n\n2. **README terminology drift**: `describeTokenSource` says \"stored in OS keychain\" (`auth.go:140-141`), `localTokenSourceName` says \"keychain\" (`auth.go:522`). On Linux there's no keychain — it's libsecret/gnome-keyring. The user-facing word is consistent at least, but \"OS keychain\" is macOS-flavored. Probably fine; flag if there's any prior convention.\n\n3. **PR description could explicitly mention** that the `--no-keyring` flag from the issue was intentionally subsumed by `ENTIRE_SECRETS_PATH` (file backend is already plaintext JSON, so a separate flag would just be a duplicate ingestion path). Right now the PR says \"does not automatically fall back to plaintext\" without naming the original ask.\n\n4. **No test for \"SECRETS_PATH set but file-write fails\"** — e.g., bad path, no write perms. The error-wrapping is fine (`store.go:39-42`), and `runLogin`'s headless hint correctly suppresses itself when SECRETS_PATH is already set (`login.go:97-99`), so the user gets the raw underlying error. Acceptable.\n\n### Verdict\n\n**Scope-appropriate, well-tested, ready for non-draft review.** The user's concern about size doesn't hold up — strip the tests and you have ~395 LOC for two new credential ingestion paths plus a source-attribution refactor across four commands. That's a reasonable density.\n\nThe main thing I'd consider before flipping out of draft: a one-sentence note in the PR body that the `--no-keyring` flag was deliberately consolidated into `ENTIRE_SECRETS_PATH`, so reviewers don't ask \"where's option 3?\"."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11724,"cache_read_input_tokens":85586,"output_tokens":5149,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11724,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":5149,"cache_read_input_tokens":85586,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11724,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11724},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CameyaN16yaR4irSwbok4","type":"assistant","uuid":"96bd1447-0374-479d-9111-1d7065b23b0e","timestamp":"2026-05-06T16:48:38.581Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/peytonmontei/Documents/entire/devenv/entireio/cli/.worktrees/issue-1036-auth-storage","sessionI